tive_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,2679,279,18509,15251,553,400,32,3,323,400,33,3,320,8206,525,537,2176,400,15,3,8,26553,279,2701,5256,3491,26107,1124,7265,90,1653,15170,66,31,6257,66,31,35702,30031,66,31,6257,66,31,6257,66,31,6257,66,92,609,609,609,609,362,609,425,62,22,3422,609,10,609,609,609,425,609,362,62,22,3422,1124,87934,90,17,12,21,92,609,609,609,362,609,362,609,220,15,62,22,3422,1124,408,90,1653,92,26107,12555,374,279,1985,315,279,18509,15251,553,400,32,3,323,400,33,3,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf the digits represented by $A$ and $B$ (which are not both $0$) satisfy the following addition problem $$ \\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & & & & A & B_7\\\\ &+ & & & B & A_7\\\\ \\cline{2-6} & & & A & A & 0_7\\\\ \\end{array} $$what is the product of the digits represented by $A$ and $B$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b27ed2962c8496890ff3a38d18f766132088acafb8a3fbf31d8ce2d920231128"},"seconds":18.5543954372406,"seed":738574825699416464,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given addition problem in base 7, we need to determine the digits \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) such that the following equation holds:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n& & & & A & B_7\\\\\n&+ & & & B & A_7\\\\\n\\cline{2-6}\n& & & A & A & 0_7\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's convert the addition problem into a base 10 equation. The number \\(ABA_7\\) in base 10 is:\n\\[\nA \\cdot 7^2 + B \\cdot 7 + A = 49A + 7B + A = 50A + 7B\n\\]\n\nThe number \\(BAA_7\\) in base 10 is:\n\\[\nB \\cdot 7^2 + A \\cdot 7 + A = 49B + 7A + A = 49B + 8A\n\\]\n\nThe sum of these two numbers in base 10 is:\n\\[\n50A + 7B + 49B + 8A = 58A + 56B\n\\]\n\nWe need this sum to equal the base 10 representation of \\(AAA_7\\), which is:\n\\[\nA \\cdot 7^2 + A \\cdot 7 + A = 49A + 7A + A = 57A\n\\]\n\nThus, we have the equation:\n\\[\n58A + 56B = 57A\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\\[\n58A + 56B = 57A \\implies A + 56B = 0 \\implies A + 56B = 57A \\implies 56B = 56A \\implies B = A\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are digits in base 7 and not both zero, the possible values for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are from 1 to 6. Let's verify if \\(A = B\\) satisfies the original addition problem:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n& & & & A & A_7\\\\\n&+ & & & A & A_7\\\\\n\\cline{2-6}\n& & & A & A & 0_7\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn base 10, this is:\n\\[\n50A + 7A + A = 58A\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n57A\n\\]\n\nSince \\(58A = 57A + A\\), the equation holds true. Therefore, \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) can be any digit from 1 to 6. The product of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\nA \\cdot A = A^2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are equal and can be any digit from 1 to 6, we can choose \\(A = B = 1\\) for simplicity. Thus, the product is:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 1 = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the digits represented by \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:316"}
